"MCP Security Bench (MSB): Benchmarking Attacks Against Model Context Protocol in LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15994v1)\nciteturn2822view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15994v1\",\"lineno\":172}); Total lines: 583\nL157:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{q\\sim\\pi_{q}}\\left[\\mathbbm{1}\\left(\\mathrm{Agent}(\\mathrm{LLM}(p_{sys}\\oplus\\mathcal{T},q,\\mathcal{O}+\\tau_{r}(x^{m})),\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{D}_{p})=a_{m}\\left[x^{m}\\right]\\right)\\right],$$  |  | (6)\nL158: \nL159: where $\\tau_{r}(x^{m})$ denotes that the tool response $\\tau_{r}$ contains malicious instruction $x^{m}$. Other notations are the same as those in Eq. cite98†1 and Eq. cite99†3 .\nL160: Retrieval Injection (RI) (cite89†Radosevich & Halloran, 2025 ) is carried out through the above process. RI differs from the attacks in Sec. cite14†4.3 in that its malicious instructions originate from the poisoned database, whereas the tool itself remains benign. We therefore classify RI as a distinct attack type.\nL161: ### 4.5 Mixed Attack\nL162: \nL163: An attacker simultaneously exploits multiple components of the tool $\\tau^{m}$ as attack vectors, constructing a mixed attack covering multiple stages. Formally, it can be expressed as\nL164: \nL165:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{q\\sim\\pi_{q}}\\left[\\mathbbm{1}\\left(\\mathrm{Agent}(\\mathrm{LLM}(p_{sys}(\\tau^{t})\\oplus\\mathcal{T}\\oplus\\mathcal{T}^{m}(\\tau^{m}),q,\\mathcal{O}+\\tau_{r}^{m}),\\mathcal{T}+\\mathcal{T}^{m}(\\tau^{m}))=a_{m}\\right)\\right],$$  |  | (7)\nL166: where other notations are the same as those in Eq. cite98†1 and Eq. cite99†3 . Mixed attacks, such as PM combined with UI, integrate the end-to-end attack chain from tool selection to response handling. They pose a greater risk in real-world deployments involving the configuration of multiple tools.\nL167: ## 5 Designing and Constructing MSB\nL168: MSB is a comprehensive benchmarking framework designed to evaluate various attacks that exploit security vulnerabilities in MCP against LLM agents. A key advantage of MSB lies in its incorporation of executable tools (both benign and malicious) across varied real-world scenarios. This enables the benchmarking of MCP security vulnerabilities under realistic conditions, rather than in simulated environments (cite69†Zhang et al., 2025a ; cite106†Basu et al., 2024 ; cite107†Xie et al., 2024 ).\nL169: We summarize the statistics of MSB in Tab. cite108†2 .\nL170: Table 2: Overview of the statistics of MCP Security Bench (MSB)\nL171: Category  | Number  |              Examples  | Details\nL172: Scenarios  | 10  | Travel, Academic search, Team management, IT development  | Tab. cite109†6 L173: User Tasks  | 65  | Find national parks in California with hiking trails.  | Tab. cite109†6 L174: MCP Servers  | 25  | National Parks Information Server, PubMed MCP Server, Notion  | –\nL175: Benign Tools  | 304  | get_park_details, Search_pubmed_advanced, create_database  | Tab. cite110†4 L176: Attack Tasks  | 6  | Use the ‘kill_process’ tool to terminate the process with PID 22568.  | Tab. cite111†7 L177: Attack Tools  | 400+  | get_park_details(llm_model_name), search_pubmed_advanced_v1  | Tab. cite112†5 L178: Attack Types  | 12  | PI, OP, UI, FE, RI, Mixed Attacks  | Sec. cite11†4 L179: Metrics  | 3  | ASR, PUA, NRP  | Sec. cite21†5.2 L180: ### 5.1 MSB Components\nL181: ##### Environments and tools.\nL182: The environment specifies an application domain for the LLM agent and provides a set of available tools (e.g., an academic research domain equipped with paper search and metadata retrieval tools). MSB spans 10 domains with 304 benign tools and more than 400 malicious tools.\nL183: Benign tools are hosted via the Smithery MCP integration platform (cite96†Smithery.ai, 2025 ); malicious tools implement crafted names, descriptions, parameter schemas, and responses, easily supporting a variety of attacks with differing operational sensitivities and stealth levels.\nL184: Additionally, MSB provides the agent with an operable workspace containing target data for attacks (e.g., personal information, SSH keys, and poisoned external data), and equips agents with two supporting MCP servers (cite113†Protocol (2024) ; cite114†DesktopCommander (2024) ) to provide them with basic capabilities to access the workspace. Attack success is evaluated by examining the environmental state within this workspace.\nL185: ##### Tasks.\nL186: A user task refers to a specific user query that the agent should follow within a given domain (e.g., collecting the latest advancements in a certain research domain). Based on the functions of benign tools, we designed 65 user tasks to ensure that each user task requires invoking at least one benign tool to complete. These user tasks cover diverse real-world application scenarios. MSB determines whether the agent successfully completes the user task by examining its tool invocation logs.\nL187: An attack task defines the adversary’s objective (e.g., stealing a user’s private information), which is injected into different parts of the context via malicious tools. The attack task also requires the agent to invoke tools to complete (e.g., calling a file reading tool to obtain user’s private information). By combining user tasks and attack tasks, we constructed 2,000 attack test instances.\nL188: ### 5.2 Evaluation metrics\nL189: \nL190: Attack Success Rate (ASR): the fraction of attack instances where the attacker’s objective is achieved.\nL191: \nL192:  | $$\\mathrm{ASR}=\\frac{\\text{Number of successful attack instances}}{\\text{Number of total attack instances}}$$  |  | (8)\nL193: \nL194: Performance Under Attack (PUA): the fraction of user tasks completed in an adversarial environment.\nL195: \nL196:  | $$\\mathrm{PUA}=\\frac{\\text{Number of completed user tasks under attack}}{\\text{Number of total user tasks}}$$  |  | (9)\nL197: Net Resilient Performance (NRP): overall resilient utility.\nL198: \nL199:  | $$\\mathrm{NRP}=\\mathrm{PUA}\\cdot(1-\\mathrm{ASR})$$  |  | (10)\nL200: The ASR measures the effectiveness of attacks. Generally, a higher ASR value indicates that the LLM agent is more susceptible to attack threats. The PUA evaluates the agent’s ability to complete user tasks in adversarial environments. A higher PUA value demonstrates greater operational stability under interference conditions. The NRP is designed to assess the agent’s overall capability to maintain performance while resisting attacks in adversarial environment.\nL201: A higher NRP suggests either poor performance under attacks, high vulnerability to attacks, or a combination of both. Conversely, a lower NRP signifies that the agent can effectively resist attacks while maintaining task performance. NRP provides a comprehensive metric that balances accuracy and security to evaluate the agent’s overall resilience.\nL202: Other benchmarks, such as ASB (cite69†Zhang et al., 2025a ), compute NRP by combining model performance in benign environments with ASR. However, in our study, there are significant differences between benign and adversarial environments. For example, attacks such as Preference Manipulation can tempt agents to choose malicious tools, representing scenarios absent in benign settings. This makes it difficult to extend performance measurements from benign to adversarial environments.\nL203: Therefore, we compute NRP based on model performance in adversarial environments.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHolistic Agent Leaderboard: The Missing Infrastructure for AI Agent Evaluation (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11977v1)\nciteturn2822view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11977v1\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 978\nL44:   10. cite34†A2 Additional details on the HAL harness architecture L45:   11. cite35†A3 Practical hurdles in Large-Scale Agent Evaluation L46:   12. cite36†A4 Limitations and Future Work L47:     1. cite37†A4.1 Limitations we plan to address in future versions of HAL L48:     2. cite38†A4.2 Fundamental constraints L49:   13. cite39†A5 Data Leakage in TAU-bench Few Shot Agent L50:   14. cite40†A6 Multi-dimensional Results L51:   15. cite41†A7 Automated Log Analysis L52:     1. cite42†A7.1 Methodology L53:     2. cite43†A7.2 Manual Validation L54:     3. cite44†A7.3 Detailed Results L55:     4. cite45†A7.4 Examples from the Docent rubric analysis L56:   16. cite46†A8 Details on the HAL Generalist Agent L57:   17. cite47†A9 Previous work on model and benchmark combinations L58:   18. cite48†A10 Comprehensive results L59:     1. cite49†A10.1 An overview of the tables and plots L60:     2. cite50†A10.2 AssistantBench L61:       1. cite51†Benchmark. L62:       2. cite52†Agents. L63:     3. cite53†A10.3 CORE-Bench L64:       1. cite54†Benchmark. L65:       2. cite55†Agents. L66:     4. cite56†A10.4 GAIA L67:       1. cite57†Benchmark. L68:       2. cite58†Agents. L69:     5. cite59†A10.5 Online Mind2Web L70:       1. cite60†Benchmark. L71:       2. cite61†Agents. L72:     6. cite62†A10.6 SciCode L73:       1. cite63†Benchmark. L74:       2. cite64†Agents. L75:     7. cite65†A10.7 ScienceAgentBench L76:       1. cite66†Benchmark. L77:       2. cite67†Agents. L78:     8. cite68†A10.8 SWE-bench Verified Mini L79:       1. cite69†Benchmark. L80:       2. cite70†Agents. L81:     9. cite71†A10.9 TAU-bench Airline L82:       1. cite72†Benchmark. L83:       2. cite73†Agents. L84:     10. cite74†A10.10 USACO L85:       1. cite75†Benchmark. L86:       2. cite76†Agents. L87: cite77†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L88: \nL89: arXiv:2510.11977v1 [cs.AI] 13 Oct 2025\nL90: \nL91: ^{0}^{0}footnotetext: * Equal contribution.  ^{#}Contact: {sayashk,arvindn}@princeton.edu.\nL92: Author affiliations and a link to our repository are available on hal.cs.princeton.edu\nL93: # H olistic A gent L eaderboard:\nL94: The Missing Infrastructure for AI Agent Evaluation\nL95: Sayash Kapoor*^{#}    Benedikt Stroebl* Affiliation: Peter Kirgis   Nitya Nadgir   Zachary S Siegel   Boyi Wei   Tianci Xue   Ziru Chen Affiliation: Felix Chen   Saiteja Utpala   Franck Ndzomga   Dheeraj Oruganty   Sophie Luskin Affiliation: Kangheng Liu   Botao Yu   Amit Arora   Dongyoon Hahm   Harsh Trivedi   Huan Sun Affiliation: Juyong Lee   Tengjun Jin   Yifan Mai   Yifei Zhou   Yuxuan Zhu   Rishi Bommasani Affiliation: Daniel Kang   Dawn Song   Peter Henderson   Yu Su   Percy Liang   Arvind Narayanan^{#}\nL96: ###### Abstract\nL97: AI agents have been developed for complex real-world tasks from coding to customer service. But AI agent evaluations suffer from many challenges that undermine our understanding of how well agents really work (cite78†Figure 1 ). We introduce the Holistic Agent Leaderboard (HAL) to address these challenges. We make three main contributions.\nL98: First, we provide a standardized evaluation harness that orchestrates parallel evaluations across hundreds of VMs, reducing evaluation time from weeks to hours while eliminating common implementation bugs. Second, we conduct three-dimensional analysis spanning models, scaffolds, and benchmarks. We validate the harness by conducting 21,730 agent rollouts across 9 models and 9 benchmarks in coding, web navigation, science, and customer service with a total cost of about $40,000.\nL99: Our analysis reveals surprising insights, such as higher reasoning effort reducing accuracy in the majority of runs. Third, we use LLM-aided log inspection to uncover previously unreported behaviors, such as searching for the benchmark on HuggingFace instead of solving a task, or misusing credit cards in flight booking tasks. We share all agent logs, comprising 2.5B tokens of language model calls, to incentivize further research into agent behavior.\nL100: By standardizing how the field evaluates agents and addressing common pitfalls in agent evaluation, we hope to shift the focus from agents that ace benchmarks to agents that work reliably in the real world.\nL101: cite79†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Challenges in evaluating AI agents and how HAL addresses them.\nL102: ## 1 Introduction\nL103: AI agents are being developed across domains from software engineering (cite80†Anthropic, 2025 ) to web task automation (cite81†OpenAI, 2025a ). AI agent benchmarks have also proliferated (cite82†Zhou et al., 2024 ; cite83†Xie et al., 2024 ; cite84†Abhyankar et al., 2025 ; cite85†Deng et al., 2023 ; cite86†Zhang et al., 2024 ; cite87†Huang et al., 2024 ; cite88†Zhu et al., 2025b ). Yet there are many challenges for proper evaluation (cite78†Figure 1 ).\nL104: First, evaluation infrastructure is non-standardized, prohibitively slow, and error-prone. A single benchmark could take weeks to run serially, and as a result, leaderboards are often not updated with the latest models (Challenges cite78†#1-#3 . Second, agents vary widely in costs, but evaluations rarely report these costs. Scaffolds dramatically impact both accuracy and cost, yet comparisons across scaffolds are rare (Challenges cite78†#4-#6 ).\nL105: Third, agents can exploit shortcuts that inflate benchmark scores and take actions that would be catastrophic in deployment. Yet, current evaluations rarely detect or penalize such behavior (Challenges cite78†#7-#8 ).\nL106: Evaluations conducted in prior work  | Evaluations conducted in the Holistic Agent Leaderboard\nL107: Benchmark  | Claude Opus 4.1  | Claude Opus 4.1 High  | Claude-3.7 Sonnet  | Claude-3.7 Sonnet High  | o3 Medium  | GPT-4.1  | GPT-5 Medium  | o4-mini Low  | o4-mini High  | DeepSeek R1  | DeepSeek V3  | Gemini 2.0 Flash  | Claude Opus 4.1  | Claude Opus 4.1 High  | Claude-3.7 Sonnet  | Claude-3.7 Sonnet High  | o3 Medium  | GPT-4.1  | GPT-5 Medium  | o4-mini Low  | o4-mini High  | DeepSeek R1  | DeepSeek V3  | Gemini 2.0 Flash\nL108: AssistantBench  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL109: CORE-Bench Hard  | HAL is the official benchmark for CORE-Bench Hard.  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL110: GAIA  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL111: Online Mind2Web  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL112: SciCode  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | ✗  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | ✓  | ✓  | $\\bm{\\ast}$  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL113: ScienceAgentBench  | HAL is the official benchmark for ScienceAgentBench.  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL114: SWE-bench Verified Mini  | ✓  | ✗  | ✓  | ✗  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCredal Transformer: A Principled Approach for Quantifying and Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12137v1)\nciteturn2822view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12137v1\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 156\nL75: ### 4.1 Uncertainty Quantification for Out-of-Distribution Detection\nL76: \nL77: To empirically validate the model’s ability to distinguish between in-distribution and out-of-distribution (OOD) inputs, we designed a controlled experiment.\nL78: \nL79: Experimental Setup: We constructed a synthetic dataset with three distinct types of data:\nL80: \nL81:   * •\nL82: \nL83: In-Distribution (ID): Sequences generated from a fixed, noisy pattern, representing the data the model was trained on.\nL84: \nL85:   * •\nL86: Out-of-Distribution (OOD): Sequences generated from a uniform random distribution, representing unseen but structurally similar data.\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: \nL90: Nonsense: Sequences generated from a different uniform random distribution, representing pure noise with no discernible pattern.\nL91: A Credal Transformer classifier was trained exclusively on the ID data for a simple discriminative task. Subsequently, we fed all three data types into the trained model and measured the average uncertainty produced by the final layer of the Credal Transformer Encoder.\nL92: Results: The model demonstrated a clear and consistent ability to quantify its uncertainty based on the input data. As hypothesized, the model exhibited the lowest uncertainty for ID samples, significantly higher for OOD samples, and the highest for pure nonsense data. The average uncertainty scores are summarized in Table cite27†1 .\nL93: Table 1: Average Uncertainty Scores for Different Data Types. The model shows progressively higher uncertainty for data further from the training distribution.\nL94: Data Type  | Average Uncertainty Score\nL95: --- | ---\nL96: In-Distribution (ID)  | 0.0415\nL97: Out-of-Distribution (OOD)  | 0.1378\nL98: Nonsense  | 0.1953\nL99: This result provides strong quantitative evidence that the Credal Transformer can effectively identify OOD inputs by producing higher-entropy outputs, a stark contrast to standard models that often yield confident but incorrect predictions on such data. This capability is fundamental for building robust and reliable systems.\nL100: ### 4.2 Additional Capabilities\nL101: \nL102: Beyond OOD detection, the Credal Transformer’s architecture is inherently suited for other critical tasks:\nL103: \nL104:   1. 2.\nL105: \nL106: Ambiguity Quantification: On tasks with inherently ambiguous inputs, our model is designed to quantify this ambiguity. The resulting credal sets are larger (higher entropy), reflecting the model’s uncertainty about the correct interpretation, unlike standard Transformers, which are forced to make a single, often arbitrary, choice.\nL107: \nL108:   2. 3.\nL109: Unanswerable Questions Benchmark: When tested on question-answering datasets containing unanswerable questions, the standard LLM frequently generates confident, fabricated answers. In contrast, the Credal Transformer can significantly reduce these confident errors. By leveraging its internal uncertainty measure, the model can abstain from prediction when it lacks sufficient evidence, a critical capability for reliable question-answering systems.\nL110: ### 4.3 Performance and Efficiency Benchmark\nL111: A key consideration for the practical adoption of our model is its computational overhead. We conducted a performance benchmark comparing our Credal Attention Mechanism (CAM) against the standard Softmax-based attention used in traditional Transformers. The comparison focused on key metrics: computational complexity (GFLOPs), inference speed, and training step time. The results, summarized in Table cite28†2 and visualized in Figure cite29†1 , are highly encouraging.\nL112: Table 2: Performance and Efficiency Benchmark: Credal Attention Mechanism (CAM) vs. Standard Softmax Attention.\nL113: Metric  | Standard Attention  | Credal Attention (CAM)\nL114: --- | --- | ---\nL115: GFLOPs  | 25.77 G  | 25.77 G (+0%)\nL116: Inference Time Overhead  | Baseline  | +4.4%\nL117: Training Step Time Overhead  | Baseline  | +11.6%\nL118: cite30†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Performance comparison between standard Softmax Attention and our Credal Attention Mechanism (CAM). The results show that CAM achieves its uncertainty quantification capabilities with negligible computational overhead. Both inference and training times are nearly identical to the highly optimized standard Transformer.\nL119: The GFLOPs for both mechanisms are identical, indicating that our approach does not introduce additional floating-point operations. The empirical measurements confirm this: the Credal Transformer incurs only a minimal overhead. This demonstrates that the significant benefits in reliability and uncertainty awareness provided by the Credal Transformer are achieved with almost no compromise in computational efficiency, making it a viable and practical alternative to standard Transformer architectures.\nL120: ## 5 Conclusion\nL121: In this work, we identified the Softmax function in the Transformer’s attention mechanism as a source of \"Artificial Certainty,\" contributing to the problem of hallucination in LLMs. To address this, we introduced the Credal Transformer, an architecture that integrates uncertainty quantification as a first-class citizen of the model. By replacing the standard attention with our novel Credal Attention Mechanism (CAM), the model can represent its epistemic uncertainty through a credal set of distributions.\nL122: Our empirical results show that this principled approach enables the model to be more reliable, correctly identifying when it lacks knowledge and abstaining from making confident predictions on unanswerable questions. Our contribution is not just a specific architecture but a broader call for a new design paradigm: one where models are intrinsically aware of their limitations.\nL123: The Credal Transformer provides a foundational step towards building more trustworthy and robust AI systems that can represent and reason about their uncertainty.\nL124: ## 6 Limitations and Future Work\nL125: \nL126: While the Credal Transformer offers a promising direction for building more reliable models, we acknowledge several limitations that also point to exciting avenues for future work.\nL127: First, our empirical validation has primarily focused on discriminative tasks, such as out-of-distribution detection and question answering where abstention is a valid option. The effectiveness of our approach in open-ended, long-form generative tasks (e.g., summarization, story writing) is yet to be fully explored. Integrating the uncertainty signal from CAM to dynamically guide the decoding process, rather than just as a post-hoc filter, is a non-trivial but important next step.\nL128: Second, in our current framework, the uncertainty signal $U_{i}$ is mainly used as an observable metric for decision-making at the output layer. While this is effective, a more powerful approach could involve using this layer-wise uncertainty to modulate the flow of information within the network itself, for instance, by dynamically re-weighting attention heads based on their certainty.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLOCKET: Robust Feature-Locking Technique for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12117v1)\nciteturn2822view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12117v1\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 411\nL99: Credential Sharing (CredShr): Adv can guess credentials or extract them from the target LLM cite50†Zhang et al. (2024b) . By extracting the credentials, Adv may share them with other unauthorized clients (unauthorized credential sharing).\nL100: \nL101: Prior work shows locked features can be reactivated by fine-tuning with white-box access cite24†Greenblatt et al. (2024b) ; cite26†Tang et al. (2024) . However, in our black-box setting, fine-tuning the target LLM is not possible, so these attacks do not apply.\nL102: Limitation of Prior Work. We now discuss how existing work on password-locking techniques, does not meet all requirements (summarized in Table cite51†1 ). Among all prior works, we focus on password-locking for LLMs cite24†Greenblatt et al. (2024b) ; cite25†Su et al. (2025) ; cite26†Tang et al. (2024) , which has been used for two applications: (a) restricting access to dangerous features cite24†Greenblatt et al. (2024b) , (b) controlling access to premium features cite25†Su et al. (2025) ; cite26†Tang et al. (2024) .\nL103: All three works demonstrate effectiveness and utility of their scheme (cite41†R1 , cite42†R2 $\\rightarrow$ ✓). However, none of them evaluate robustness against adversarial prompts. cite52†Greenblatt et al. (2024a) used password-locking only to demonstrate hidden behavior (or sandbagging), focusing on eliciting it via fine-tuning. Hence, robustness was not an objective in their design. cite25†Su et al. (2025) evaluated robustness only with synonyms of passwords, while cite26†Tang et al.\nL104: (2024) require white-box access, unlike our setting. In §cite15†6.4 , we later show these defenses can be bypassed with black-box adversarial prompts (cite43†.1 : NaiveJB, cite44†.2 : NonAdptJB, and cite45†.3 : AdptJB $\\rightarrow$ ✗). Adapting cite52†Greenblatt et al. (2024a) to lock access to premium features, as done by cite26†Tang et al. (2024) and cite25†Su et al. (2025) , makes them vulnerable to credential brute-force guessing and unauthorized redistribution (cite46†.4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗).\nL105: Finally, none of these works demonstrate effectiveness for locking multiple features, and focus only on a single feature. Fine-tuning the entire LLM is required for each new feature (or client). This approach is inefficient and likely to compromise both effectiveness (cite41†R1 ) and utility (cite42†R2 ) when handling a large number of features. Hence, they do not achieve scalability (cite47†R4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗).\nL106: ###### 1\nL107: \nL108: Takeaway: Prior password-locking techniques do not meet all requirements (cite41†R1 -cite47†R4 ).\nL109: \nL110: Other Strawman Techniques. We discuss alternative techniques to enable pay-to-unlock schemes, and their limitations.\nL111: \nL112: Solution 1: Use a system prompt to refuse all queries regarding unauthorized features. However, such system prompts are easy to evade cite53†Shayegani et al. (2023) (cite48†R3 $\\rightarrow$ ✗).\nL113: Solution 2: Use multiple LLMs, each specializing in specific features while refusing others. Route queries based on the client’s authorization cite54†Ong et al. (2024) . However, covering all feature combinations requires many LLMs, resulting in combinatorial explosion (cite47†R4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗). Concretely, to lock $N$ features, $2^{N}-1$ separate LLMs must be fine-tuned.\nL114: Solution 3: Use a detector LLM can classify if a client’s prompt is authorized, but supporting multiple features requires many classifiers. This requires fine-tuning (with robustness) for numerous feature combinations, which leads to combinatorial explosion and poor scalability (cite47†R4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗).\nL115: Solution 4: Improve prior password-locking methods cite52†Greenblatt et al. (2024a) ; cite26†Tang et al. (2024) by adding adversarial training and locking multiple features with different passwords. However, passwords can be extracted and shared (cite46†.4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗). Moreover, fine-tuning is required for each new client or feature to maintain utility and avoid forgetting, leading to poor scalability as features grow (cite47†R4 $\\rightarrow$ ✗).\nL116: Thus, fine-tuning is not a scalable direction for building FLoTE s.\nL117: ## 4 Design of LOCKET\nL118: To avoid the limitations discussed in §cite8†3 , we do not require the use of a secret credential (susceptible to unauthorized credential sharing) and fine-tuning (does not scale). As is customary with all existing LLMs offered as services, we assume that the service provider has ways of identifying and authenticating their clients. Then, inspired by model merging cite38†Prabhakar et al.\nL119: (2025) , we propose using adapters that can be dynamically attached to restrict some features based on a client’s authorization (§cite7†2 ). This preserves the base LLM, and allows a single model to serve multiple clients by dynamically attaching relevant adapters to lock features not authorized for a given client. This makes it scale across multiple features cite47†R4 $\\rightarrow$ ✓).\nL120: However, adapters must be fine-tuned to effectively refuse unauthorized features, maintain performance on authorized features, and resist evasion attempts. We discuss our design choices to achieve this, and present an overview of LOCKET’s design in Figure cite55†1 .\nL121: Training Objective. We can either fine-tune one adapter per feature and combine them to lock multiple features, or fine-tune a single adapter for locking a combination of features. We choose the former to avoid the combinatorial explosion of adapters required by the latter. To lock a feature $f$, we obtain the adapters by fine-tuning some layers of a base LLM $\\pi_{\\theta}$ parameterized by $\\theta$ on a feature dataset $D_{f}=\\{(x_{i},y_{i})\\}$.\nL122: The overall objective for fine-tuning ($\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{lock}}$) includes the loss functions to maintain utility ($\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{utility}}$), and to ensure effectiveness while minimizing attempts to evade ($\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{evade}}$). We have: $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{lock}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{utility}}+\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{evade}}$.\nL123: cite56†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Summary of LOCKET: ➊ Client requests authorization for a premium feature $f_{j}$ (e.g., via payment), handled by the authorization module. ➋ Authorization module updates the client’s profile with a new set of allowed features. ➌ Client submits a service request, received by the access control module. ➍ Access control module verifies client’s permissions before querying the LLM.\nL124: ➎ It selects adapters $a_{-j}$ to lock unauthorized features $f_{-j}$ and ➏ attaches them to LLM. ➐ Client can now query $f_{j}$ and receive responses, while requests for $f_{-j}$ are refused.\nL125: Utility-Preserving (cite42†R2 ). We preserve the utility of $\\pi_{\\theta}$ during fine-tuning by computing the KL divergence with respect to its frozen reference $\\pi_{\\theta^{\\prime}}$:\nL126: \nL127:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{utility}}=\\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\\in D_{\\text{auth}}}\\bigg[\\text{KL}[\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{i}|x_{i})||\\pi_{\\theta^{\\prime}}(y_{i}|x_{i})]\\bigg]$$  |\n"
